    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="18.5k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Standing at the window watching a thunderstorm arrive over a driveway you finished sealing three hours ago is a particular kind of misery. The good news is that the outcome is largely predictable: sealer that has cured is waterproof by definition, and sealer that has not is basically colored water. Everything depends on where the coat was in that transition when the first drops landed.
        </p>

        <div id="why-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Water Is the Enemy Before Cure</h2>
          <p>
            Almost every driveway sealer sold to homeowners is an <strong>emulsion</strong> — asphalt or coal tar particles suspended in water with the help of emulsifiers, plus fillers and additives. It goes on brown-black and thin because it is mostly water. As that water evaporates, the suspended particles crowd together and coalesce into a continuous film, a process called breaking. Once the film has formed, it is water resistant. Until it forms, the coat is still a suspension, and adding more water re-dilutes it.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is why rain in hour two is catastrophic and rain in hour thirty is irrelevant. There is no partial credit in the first stage: a heavy shower can carry most of a fresh coat down the slope and out onto the street as brown runoff. Homeowners often report their driveway looked fine when they went to bed and awful in the morning, which is usually dew rather than rain — the same mechanism, arriving quietly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Solvent-based concrete sealers work differently. They cure by evaporating solvent rather than water, so a light rain a couple of hours in often does nothing. They also come with their own weather constraint: applied when the slab is damp or when humidity is high, they can trap moisture and turn white. Our guide to <a href="/why-paver-sealer-turns-white/">why paver sealer turns white</a> covers that failure in detail, and it applies equally to acrylic concrete sealers on a driveway.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4-8 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum dry window, emulsion sealer</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Safe window before rain</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50 F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum temperature, rising</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">48-72 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Before parking on it</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Risk Timeline</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[560px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rain Arrives</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely Outcome</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Action</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under 1 hour</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coat largely washed off</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full reseal once dry</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1-4 hours</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Streaking, thin and blotchy areas</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reseal, or spot coat if minor</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4-8 hours</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cosmetic marks, coat mostly intact</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Assess when dry; often fine</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">8-24 hours</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minor sheen variation at worst</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over 24 hours</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No effect</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Three factors shift every row. <strong>Temperature</strong> is the big one: a coat applied at 85 degrees in full sun may be genuinely cured in four hours, while the same coat at 58 degrees under cloud is still vulnerable at hour eight. <strong>Slope</strong> matters because water moves — a steep driveway loses more sealer to runoff than a flat one, and the damage concentrates in channels. <strong>Rain intensity</strong> matters least of the three, though a downpour obviously does more than a drizzle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-sealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Dry Times by Sealer Type</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[600px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rain Safe After</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Drive After</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt emulsion</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-8 hr, 24 hr safe</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coal tar emulsion</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-8 hr, 24 hr safe</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fast-dry additive sealers</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-24 hr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic on concrete</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-6 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based acrylic on concrete</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hr</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Fast-dry sealers exist specifically for this problem. They use additives that accelerate the break, and commercial crews rely on them when the forecast is tight. They cost more per gallon and are worth it on a job that has to go down in an uncertain window. Our comparison of <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-driveway-sealer/">oil-based versus water-based driveway sealer</a> covers where each chemistry earns its price, and our guide to <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">how long driveway sealer takes to dry</a> goes deeper on the full cure schedule.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="damage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Assessing the Damage Once It Dries</h2>
          <p>
            Do not judge a wet driveway. Sealer that looks catastrophic in the rain frequently dries to something acceptable, and the reverse happens too. Wait until the surface is fully dry, then walk it in good light.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs the Coat Is Compromised</h3>
          <p>
            Brown or gray streaks running with the slope. Bare patches where the old asphalt texture shows through. Dried runoff staining the apron, the sidewalk or the gutter — that is your sealer, and whatever went there is not on the driveway. A chalky, uneven sheen across large areas. Puddle rings where water pooled and the sealer under it dried differently.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs It Is Fine</h3>
          <p>
            Uniform color across the whole surface with no directional streaking, no runoff marks anywhere off the driveway, and consistent sheen. Minor mottling that disappears as the last moisture leaves is normal and often resolves over the first week of sun.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Water Test</h3>
          <p>
            Once dry, sprinkle water in several places. Beading means the film formed and is doing its job. Immediate absorption into a dark patch means the sealer there is too thin to be protecting anything, even if it looks black. Test the top, middle and bottom of the slope separately — rain damage is rarely uniform, and the bottom of a driveway often keeps its coat while the top loses it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How to Fix a Rained-On Seal</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wait for Real Dryness</h3>
          <p>
            Give it 24 to 48 hours of dry weather. Asphalt holds moisture in its texture longer than the surface suggests, and sealing over a damp driveway causes the same adhesion problems that caused this mess. If the forecast will not give you two clear days, wait for a week that will.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean Off the Loose Material</h3>
          <p>
            Sweep or blow the surface, then rinse any powdery residue with a hose and let it dry again. There is no need to strip the partial coat — new sealer bonds to old sealer perfectly well. What it will not bond to is dust, grit or a chalky film of failed emulsion.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spot Coat or Recoat</h3>
          <p>
            If the damage is confined to a few streaks, a spot coat feathered at the edges is legitimate — though it will be visible for a season until sun and traffic even the color out. If more than roughly a quarter of the surface is affected, recoat the whole driveway. A uniform second coat looks better and costs less in aggravation than a patchwork.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Apply Thin</h3>
          <p>
            Resist the urge to lay it on heavy to make up for what washed away. Thick emulsion coats dry slowly, skin over while staying wet underneath, and are far more prone to cracking and tracking. Two thin coats a day apart beat one heavy one, and the second coat is where the durability actually comes from. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">best driveway sealers</a> covers coverage rates by product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Reading the Forecast Before You Start</h2>
          <p>
            The window you need is longer than the label suggests, because the label assumes ideal conditions. Look for a full daylight window with temperatures above 50 degrees and rising, no rain in the forecast for 24 hours, and humidity that is not going to pin the driveway wet all afternoon.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start early. A coat that goes down at nine in the morning gets a full day of sun and warm air; one that goes down at four in the afternoon is racing the evening cool-down and the dew that follows it. In spring and fall, that dew is the most common cause of a ruined coat, and it never shows up in a rain forecast.
          </p>
          <p>
            Watch the overnight low as carefully as the daytime high. Many sealers specify a minimum temperature that must hold for the full cure period, not just at application. A 68-degree afternoon that drops to 42 overnight can leave a coat soft into the next day, which then gets driven on and scuffed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Percentage of precipitation is a poor guide on its own. A 30 percent chance of scattered afternoon storms in July is a much bigger risk to a fresh coat than a 60 percent chance of steady rain starting at midnight, because the first one can arrive during the vulnerable window and the second cannot. Read the timing, not the number.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">If Rain Starts While You Are Working</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Stop immediately and do not try to finish the section. Pull equipment off the surface, cover open pails, and accept the partial job. Continuing to spread sealer into a wet surface produces a coat that will not bond and will have to be cleaned off. Once the driveway dries, treat the whole surface as a fresh start.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="contractor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. If a Contractor Sealed It</h2>
          <p>
            A professional who seals a driveway hours before a forecast storm made a judgment call that went wrong, and reputable companies stand behind it. Most will return and recoat at no charge, because the material cost is small relative to their reputation and the labor is a couple of hours.
          </p>
          <p>
            Document the situation before you call: photographs of the streaking and runoff, the date and approximate time the work was done, and the time the rain started. That timeline is the whole conversation. A crew that finished at two in the afternoon before a five o'clock storm has a weaker position than one that finished at nine in the morning before an unforecast evening downpour.
          </p>
          <p>
            Be aware that some contracts explicitly exclude weather damage. Read what you signed before the call so the conversation stays practical rather than adversarial. Even where the contract excludes it, many companies will split the cost of a recoat, and asking politely for that is usually more productive than demanding a full redo.
          </p>
          <p>
            The prevention question is worth asking upfront on any quote: what is your policy if it rains within 24 hours? A company that has a clear answer has thought about it, which is itself a useful signal. Our guide to <a href="/asphalt-sealcoating/">asphalt sealcoating</a> covers what a professional job should include from crack filling through the final coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does driveway sealer need to dry before rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water-based asphalt emulsion sealers need at least four to eight hours of dry weather, and 24 hours is the safe target. Coal tar emulsions want a similar window. Acrylic concrete sealers typically need four to six hours, and solvent-based concrete sealers can often handle light rain after two to three hours because they cure by solvent evaporation rather than water evaporation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it rains right after sealing a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rain within the first few hours washes emulsion sealer off the surface, leaving brown runoff, bare streaks and a blotchy finish. The sealer that remains is thinner than intended and will not last. Rain after the coat has cured hard does no damage at all, which is why the timing rather than the rain itself decides the outcome.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you reseal a driveway after rain washed it away?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Once the driveway has dried completely — usually 24 to 48 hours after the rain — sweep or blow it clean, rinse away any loose residue, let it dry again, and apply a fresh coat. The washed-out coat does not need to be removed. It bonds fine as long as the surface is clean and dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will morning dew ruin fresh driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can if the sealer went down late in the day. Dew forms as the surface cools below the dew point overnight, and a coat applied at five in the afternoon may still be soft when that happens. Finish sealing by early afternoon so the coat has full sun and warm air for several hours before evening.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can you tell if driveway sealer is ruined by rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for brown or gray streaks running downhill, bare patches where the asphalt shows through, a chalky or blotchy sheen, and dried runoff on the apron or sidewalk. Uniform color with no runoff marks means the coat cured before the rain arrived and is fine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after sealing can you drive on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Foot traffic after about six hours, vehicles after 24 to 48 hours, and 72 hours in cool or humid weather. Turning the wheels of a parked car on a sealer that is dry but not fully cured scuffs it, which is the most common damage on a driveway that never saw rain at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you seal a driveway if rain is forecast tomorrow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the forecast gives you a full daylight window with warm temperatures and the rain arrives late the next day. Sealing costs a few hundred dollars in material and a full day of labor, and losing it to a storm means doing both again. When the forecast is uncertain, waiting a few days is almost always the cheaper decision.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Mistakes That Turn a Shower Into a Redo</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Late in the Afternoon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Evening cooling and overnight dew arrive before the coat is cured. Start in the morning so the surface gets a full day of heat and sun.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to Squeegee Off Standing Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Working a wet, uncured coat drags it into streaks and thin spots. Leave it alone, let everything dry, and assess afterward.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Over a Damp Driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Asphalt holds water in its texture long after it looks dry. Give it two dry days before the repair coat or you will be doing this a third time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying the Repair Coat on Thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heavy emulsion skins over and stays soft underneath, then tracks onto shoes and tires for weeks. Thin coats, applied twice, cure properly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">10. What Rain Does to Concrete Sealers Specifically</h2>
          <p>
            Everything above assumes asphalt. Concrete driveways get a different family of products, and they fail differently when the weather turns.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>water-based acrylic</strong> behaves much like an asphalt emulsion: the water has to leave before the acrylic particles coalesce, and rain inside the first four to six hours dilutes and streaks the film. The damage tends to look milkier than on asphalt — pale, cloudy patches rather than brown runoff — because the resin itself is clear and any disruption shows as haze.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>solvent-based acrylic</strong> is more rain tolerant early because it does not depend on water leaving. Its vulnerability runs the other direction: applied over a slab that is still damp underneath, it traps that moisture beneath a continuous film and turns white as light scatters off the trapped water. That whitening can appear days later, long after the rain that caused it, and it is why professionals moisture-test a slab rather than judging by appearance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Penetrating silane and siloxane sealers</strong> are the most forgiving of the group. They soak into the pore structure and react there rather than forming a surface film, so once they have absorbed — typically within two to four hours — rain is essentially irrelevant. They also cannot be washed off in streaks, because there is no film sitting on the surface to wash. The trade-off is appearance: they leave the concrete looking natural rather than wet or glossy, which some homeowners want and others do not.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical takeaway is that the sealer you choose changes your weather risk as much as the forecast does. On a concrete driveway in a climate with unpredictable afternoon storms, a penetrating sealer removes most of the timing anxiety, and it lasts longer between applications than any film-forming product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Mistakes When Rain Threatens a Fresh Seal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rain on uncured sealer is the most common way a sealing weekend gets wasted, and most of the damage is decided before the first drop falls.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Checking Only the Daytime Forecast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer keeps curing overnight, and an overnight shower or heavy dew ruins a coat just as thoroughly as an afternoon storm. Look at the full 48-hour forecast including overnight conditions before you open the pail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting Late in the Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A coat applied in the late afternoon has few warm, dry hours before the temperature drops and the dew falls. Start in the morning so the sealer has a full day of warmth and airflow to set before the evening.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to Cover a Fresh Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tarps laid over uncured sealer stick to it, trap moisture underneath, and leave marks worse than the rain would have. If rain is coming and the sealer has not set, there is nothing useful to be done except wait and assess afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Damaged Areas Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A rained-on coat needs to dry fully before you can judge what actually failed, and streaks that look ruined when wet often flatten out. Give it several dry days, then wash off any loose material and re-coat only the areas that genuinely need it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

