        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Rain after driveway sealing</strong> is only a disaster inside the first four to eight hours. Past that, an emulsion coat has broken into a continuous film and water does nothing. Judge the outcome after everything dries, not during the storm — brown runoff on the apron and directional streaking are the signs that matter.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you do have to redo it, the fix is straightforward: <strong class="text-white">two dry days, a clean sweep, and a thin fresh coat</strong>. And next time, start in the morning with 24 clear hours ahead of you, because no sealer chemistry beats simply picking a better day.
          </p>
